#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:1
msgid "The schedule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:3
msgid ""
"*Implemented.* A sequence of **steps**, each naming a set of page "
"classes. It is what turns a pool of classified pages into an order of "
"work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:6
msgid "Steps exist for one reason: feeding the next step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:7
msgid "**The results of one step are the input filter of the next.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:9
msgid ""
"That is what makes a two-stage extraction expressible. Step one finds the"
" funds; step two finds the managers **of those funds**, because what step"
" one found is what step two filters on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:20
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"In the formats repository this is the `Filter next iteration` column: "
"classes accumulate into the current step until a row raises the flag, "
"which closes that step and opens a new one "
"({doc}`../../guides/formats/repository`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:24
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Without steps the same thing would have to be expressed as an ordering "
"constraint between pages, which is precisely what {doc}`pages` exists to "
"avoid. Steps put the dependency between *stages*, where it is real, "
"instead of between *pages*, where it would be a lie."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:28
msgid "`FilterData`: what a step hands the next one"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:29
msgid ""
"`FilterData` — what a pipe is given besides the page — is an **enum**, "
"not a struct with two fields:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "Variant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "Available"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "at the **first** step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "the target companies, and only those"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "at **later** steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md
msgid "the accumulated results of all preceding steps, and only those"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:36
msgid ""
"The two are never available at once. An enum says so; a struct with two "
"optional fields would suggest they might both be there, and every pipe "
"would have to handle a combination that cannot occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:41
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Accepted consequence, stated rather than hidden**: a pipe that needs "
"the *target companies* works only when scheduled in the first step. This "
"is a real limit and it is recorded in {doc}`limits` rather than papered "
"over — the alternative was carrying both kinds of filter data everywhere,"
" which would make every pipe pay for a case almost none of them want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:47
msgid "Step order is insertion order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:48
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Steps preserve the order they were declared in and deduplicate. Iterating"
" them in hash order would make the order of processing — and therefore "
"the order of the output — unpredictable, and reproducible tests "
"impossible. See {doc}`determinism`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:52
msgid "A class in two steps accumulates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:54
msgid ""
"If a page class appears in two steps, its pages are processed **twice** "
"and the results **add up** rather than the later step overwriting the "
"earlier one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/schedule.md:57
msgid ""
"In the ordinary case — one class, one step — the two behaviours are "
"indistinguishable. They differ exactly where overwriting would discard "
"data that a step had already produced *and already fed forward* to the "
"step doing the overwriting. Accumulating is the only choice that cannot "
"lose work that something else already depended on."
msgstr ""

